  "problems": [
    {"id":"stream-checkpoint","title":"Stream checkpoint","difficulty":"Easy","domain":"software-development","prompt":"A service receives a sequence of event IDs that may repeat. Return the first ID that appears for the second time, or null if none repeats. Explain behavior for an empty stream.","hints":["Track IDs already seen.","Return as soon as an ID is encountered again.","State expected time and space complexity."],"solution":"Scan once with a set. Before adding each ID, check whether it is already present. Return the repeated ID immediately; if the scan finishes, return null. This is O(n) expected time and O(n) additional space.","complexity":"O(n) expected time, O(n) space","companies":["amazon","google","microsoft","flipkart"]},
    {"id":"meeting-windows","title":"Meeting windows","difficulty":"Medium","domain":"software-development","prompt":"Given sorted, non-overlapping meeting intervals and a requested duration, return the earliest available interval within a workday [start, end). Intervals are half-open. Return null if none fits.","hints":["Check the gap before the first meeting.","Then inspect each gap between adjacent intervals and the final gap.","Be explicit about whether an exact-duration fit counts."],"solution":"Maintain a cursor at the workday start. For each meeting, compare its start minus cursor with the requested duration. If it fits, return [cursor, cursor + duration). Otherwise advance the cursor to the meeting end. Check the final gap. Half-open intervals allow an exact fit.","complexity":"O(m) time, O(1) extra space","companies":["amazon","google","microsoft"]},
    {"id":"course-prerequisites","title":"Course prerequisites","difficulty":"Medium","domain":"core-cs-dsa","prompt":"A student can take a course only after completing its prerequisites. Given course count and prerequisite pairs, determine whether all courses can be completed. Explain what a cycle means.","hints":["Model courses as a directed graph.","A valid order exists exactly when the graph is acyclic.","Use DFS coloring or Kahn's indegree algorithm."],"solution":"Build adjacency lists and indegrees. Add all zero-indegree courses to a queue, repeatedly remove one and decrement its neighbors. Count processed courses. All courses can be completed when the count equals the course count; otherwise a directed cycle blocks progress.","complexity":"O(V + E) time, O(V + E) space","companies":["amazon","google","microsoft","flipkart"]},
    {"id":"weekly-active","title":"Weekly active learners","difficulty":"Easy","domain":"data-analyst","prompt":"A table has learner_id and activity_date. Write a query returning each week and the number of distinct learners active that week. State how you handle the week boundary and timezone.","hints":["Normalize timestamps to the reporting timezone first.","Truncate activity_date to a week bucket.","Count distinct learner IDs per bucket."],"solution":"Convert activity timestamps to the agreed reporting timezone, then group by the database's week-truncation function and count distinct learner_id. Document the chosen week start and exclude or handle null timestamps explicitly.","complexity":"O(n log n) typical with a grouping sort; storage depends on the query plan","companies":["amazon","google","flipkart"]},
    {"id":"retry-policy","title":"Bounded retry policy","difficulty":"Medium","domain":"cloud-devops","prompt":"Design a retry policy for a client calling a service that may return transient failures. Describe which failures are retryable, backoff, maximum attempts and how you prevent duplicate side effects.","hints":["Do not retry every error or retry forever.","Use exponential backoff with jitter and a hard deadline.","Consider idempotency for operations that mutate state."],"solution":"Retry only classified transient errors such as selected timeouts or rate limits. Use capped exponential backoff with jitter, a bounded attempt count and an overall deadline. Make mutating requests idempotent with an idempotency key, log attempts, and surface a final actionable error.","complexity":"N/A: system-design reasoning","companies":["amazon","google","microsoft"]},
    {"id":"star-example","title":"A project setback","difficulty":"Practice","domain":"aptitude-hr","prompt":"Tell a concise story about a project setback. Explain the situation, your responsibility, the actions you personally took, the measurable outcome, and what you learned. Use only a real example.","hints":["Keep context brief and specific.","Separate your actions from the team's actions.","End with an outcome and a learning you can support."],"solution":"Use STAR: briefly describe the situation and task, focus most of the answer on your concrete actions, then give an honest result and reflection. If the result was mixed, explain what you changed afterward.","complexity":"N/A: communication practice","companies":["tcs","infosys","wipro","accenture"]}
  ]
